supersymmetric theories possess remarkable properties on the quantum level . these properties are provided by non - renormalization theorems according to which supersymmetric theories have a much better ultraviolet behavior than the non - supersymmetric ones . the most famous example is , certainly , the @xmath5 , @xmath6 supersymmetric yang  mills ( sym ) theory , which is finite conformal invariant quantum field theory model @xcite . perturbative quantum corrections in @xmath5 , @xmath0 sym theories are finite starting from the two - loop approximation @xcite . @xmath5 , @xmath7 supersymmetric theories have less number of independent renormalization constants in comparison with the non - supersymmetric ones and the superpotential has no quantum corrections @xcite . moreover , it is possible to find an expression for a @xmath2-function of @xmath1 supersymmetric theories , which is exact in all orders @xcite . ( for theories containing chiral matter superfields this expression relates the @xmath2-function with the anomalous dimension of the matter superfields . ) this expression for the @xmath2-function is called the exact novikov , shifman , vainshtein , and zakharov ( nsvz ) @xmath2-function . the most elegant approach to the non - renormalization theorems is obtained in the framework of the superfield formulation of supersymmetric theories . in this case , the proof of the non - renormalization theorems is based on three points : ( i ) superspace structure of superpropagators , containing the delta - functions of anticommuting variables that allow to shrink the loops into dots in @xmath8 space , ( ii ) the superfield background field method for supersymmetric gauge theories , and ( iii ) an assumption about existence of a regularization manifestly preserving the supersymmetry ( see e.g. @xcite ) . first two points are realized in the explicit form . as to the last one , it is not very clear from the beginning , how to construct a regularization which preserves the supersymmetry and which will be convenient for practical computations of supergraphs ( see e.g. @xcite ) . therefore , a part of the proofs of the non - renormalization theorems based on the above assumption needs an additional justification . it is known that the usually used dimensional regularization @xcite , explicitly breaks the supersymmetry ( see e.g. @xcite ) , because numbers of bosonic and fermionic degrees of freedom differently depend on the space - time dimension . most calculations of quantum corrections in supersymmetric theories are done using the regularization by the dimensional reduction @xcite , which is a special modification of the dimensional regularization , and the @xmath9-scheme . using @xmath9-scheme the finiteness of the @xmath6 sym theory was verified by explicit calculations in one- @xcite , two- @xcite , three- @xcite , and four - loop @xcite approximations . vanishing of two- and three - loop contributions to the @xmath2-function of the @xmath10 sym theory was explicitly demonstrated in @xcite . the @xmath2-function for a general @xmath7 sym theory was calculated in one- @xcite , two- @xcite , three- @xcite , and four - loop @xcite approximations . ( the result agrees with the exact nsvz @xmath2-function only in the one- and two - loop approximations , where a @xmath2-function is scheme - independent . in the higher orders the nsvz @xmath2-function can be obtained only after a specially constructed finite renormalization @xcite . ) however , it is known that the dimensional reduction is not consistent from the mathematical point of view @xcite . due to this inconsistency any @xmath11 supersymmetry can be broken by quantum corrections in higher loops @xcite . this means that the non - renormalization theorems are not completely justified in framework of dimensional reduction and the problem of their justification is in general open . other methods can be also used @xcite for calculations of quantum corrections . in principle , it is possible even to use non - invariant regularizations , if a subtraction scheme is tuned in such a way that the slavnov taylor identities are valid for the renormalized effective action @xcite . however , for practical purposes it is much better to use an invariant regularization . moreover , the existence of a regularization which preserves supersymmetries of a theory is a key step for proving the non - renormalization theorems . in order to construct an invariant regularization it is convenient to formulate a theory in terms of @xmath1 superfields , because in this case @xmath1 supersymmetry is a manifest symmetry . a mathematically consistent invariant regularization , which does not break @xmath1 supersymmetry , is the higher covariant derivative regularization @xcite . in the supersymmetric case it can be formulated in terms of @xmath7 superfields @xcite and , therefore , does not break @xmath1 supersymmetry . in addition to the supersymmetric regularization , manifestly supersymmetric quantization of a theory also requires supersymmetric gauge fixing procedure . in the case of @xmath1 supersymmetric theories structure of divergences can be studied either using the component fields in the wess  zumino gauge , or using the superfield formulation . in the latter case the gauge can be fixed without breaking @xmath1 supersymmetry @xcite and the quantum corrections are calculated in a manifestly @xmath1 supersymmetric way that makes this procedure very convenient . application of the higher covariant derivative regularization ( complemented by a supersymmetric gauge condition ) to calculation of quantum corrections for @xmath1 supersymmetric theories allows to explain naturally the origin of the exact nsvz @xmath2-function . loop integrals for the @xmath2-function appear to be integrals of total derivatives @xcite and even integrals of double total derivatives @xcite . a qualitative explanation of this fact can be given by analyzing feynman rules @xcite using a method proposed in @xcite . because the integrands in integrals which determine a @xmath2-function are total derivatives , at least one of the loop integrals can be calculated analytically . this gives the nsvz relation between the @xmath2-function and the anomalous dimension which are defined in terms of the bare coupling constant @xcite . for the @xmath1 supersymmetric electrodynamics this was proved in all orders @xcite . as a consequence , in the abelian case the nsvz @xmath2-function was obtained exactly in all orders of the perturbation theory for the renormalization group functions defined in terms of the bare coupling constant . if the renormalization group functions are defined in terms of the renormalized coupling constant , the nsvz @xmath2-function is obtained in a special subtraction scheme , which can be naturally constructed if the theory is regularized by higher covariant derivatives @xcite . this ( nsvz ) scheme is obtained by imposing the boundary conditions ( [ nsvz_scheme ] ) on the renormalization constants . however , so far there is no proof that in non - abelian theories the exact nsvz @xmath2-function is obtained with the higher covariant derivative regularization in all orders . nevertheless , arguments based on anomalies @xcite and explicit calculations in the lowest loops @xcite allow to suggest this . therefore , using the invariant regularization for @xmath1 supersymmetric theories it is possible to make general conclusions concerning the structure of divergences . existence of an invariant regularization is also needed for proving that the @xmath2-function of @xmath0 sym theories vanishes beyond the one - loop approximation @xcite . the higher covariant derivative regularization is formulated in terms of @xmath1 superfields . certainly , @xmath0 sym theories can be written in terms of @xmath1 superfields ( see e.g. @xcite ) . however , in this case only @xmath1 supersymmetry is manifest , the second supersymmetry being hidden and on - shell